00:00:01.439 --> 00:00:03.510 align:start position:0%
 
eu leaders meet tonight to discuss  uh new sanctions against belarus after  authorities in  minsk forced a ryan airplane to land  and arrested a passenger on board exiled  belarusian activist  roman protestevic was traveling from  greece to lithuania  when the aircraft was diverted to  belarus escorted by a fighter jet under  the pretense of a security alert  the incident has sparked global outrage  with western leaders describing it as a  hijacking and  aviation piracy ireland and ryanair say  security agents were on flight  and left it after it touched down in  minsk belarus  rejects the accusations as unfounded  the crowd at vilnius airport waiting for  hours  for a plane that finally arrived  without roman protozovich on board a  message from the regime in belarus  critics aren't safe anywhere even in the  air  i am we are roman protozovich solidarity  for the blogger and activist who now  faces up to 15 years in prison  pratosovich was living in exile he's a  co-founder of nichta  an activist channel on the telegram  messaging app used to help organize  protests against the belarusian  government  on sunday the ryanair flight he was on  had almost reached vilnius  when it was forced to make a u-turn and  head with military escort seen here  to minsk passengers described an  hours-long ordeal on the ground that  started off with belarusian police  taking protozovic into custody they just  came  and they  first of all they took the i think they  took the  the guy the journalist out from the  plane  and then they started disembarking the  passengers  european and u.s diplomats quickly  condemned the arrest as well as the  diversion of the plane  eu commission president ursula fonda  lyon tweeted the outrageous and illegal  behavior of the regime in belarus will  have consequences  she continued those responsible for the  ryanair hijacking must be sanctioned  protozoa's nista colleagues have also  called for sanctions  international pressure should be  increased the protests will not help  people cannot fight guns with stones  there should be economic pressure  a boycott of the regime european  politicians should finally  ask what would be effective  european leaders are discussing  suspending transport links between  belarus and the eu  as well as suspending belarusian  overflights  other sanctions are in the works an  effort to make the regime pay  for its defiance of international and  human rights law  let's get the latest from the belarusian  capital journalist hannah lubiakova  joins us from minsk  welcome to dw and what has been heard of  roman  protozovich since he left the plane  so what we know now uh we do not know  know much to be honest but he  is being held at the kgb prison most  probably  there is no information about him there  and the lawyer cannot reach him  the lawyer is not allowed to to meet him  and talk to his defendant so we do not  know what's happening behind  the walls of the prison um there are  currently  three criminal cases that were launched  against roman bandarenko in belarus  um we do not know whether his kind of  the fact that he was on the kgb  terrorist list was  anyhow um qualified for any terrorist  accusations against him  so this is not known yet um  and basically the the authorities in  belarus are kind of  silent about the situation with rahman  because i think  the situation kind of the effect of his  arrest  was something that they did not expect  and now they're trying to  to kind of hide silence this information  right uh the state media there in  belarus reporting that the kidnap order  came from the president himself  why is this one 26 year old so important  to him  well indeed lukashenko said that he  himself ordered to  kind of race a fighter jet to escort the  plane to the airport in minsk  rahman bandarenka  by the kgb since november last year  he's a prominent journalist he's a  prominent blogger he's been very  active on social media and he has helped  to spread information about  the protests about police brutality  about the situation in the country  via this influential telegram channel  called  nexta and that's kind of the most  important telegram channel in the  country and was  crucial it was basically a key  um played a key role during the protest  in august when there was no internet at  all  and telegram was only partly accessible  so uh everybody knows about rahman  pradeshivish and bowarus and he  um he has been a prominent journalist  and and kind of people are really  concerned about  his safety at the moment and what's  happening with him right now  thank you for that uh thanks for joining  us journalist hannah lubiakova  in minsk  let's get more on the eu reaction to  this incident from dw's at brussels  bureau chief alexandra fun norman  welcome  alexandra so the eu leaders meeting  tonight  to discuss the bloc's reaction um what's  on the table  an array of potential punitive measures  that is what's on the table some member  states  such as lithuania or poland are saying  that it's what's needed is a non-fly  zone over belarus  they are calling on suspending all  european union flights  over belarus airspace they are also in  favor of  banning the belarusian state-owned  airline from  landing at european union airport  and france has always come come forward  with  a sort of proposal to also discuss  suspending all traffic including  ground transit uh from belarus  to the european union something that  would have a major effect on the the  country's economy  and we will see what they are able to  decide  but that's it's totally visible that  they are  under a lot of pressure to act after  they have chosen a very strong language  to condemn  the incident in minsk so does that  pressure mean that we are likely to see  a unified reaction  well the decision today will definitely  be another  test for the blocks foreign policy that  was struggling in the past last week  the european union wanted to speak with  one voice  and to release a statement on the  israel gaza a crisis that was not  possible because hungary blocked that  statement  hungary blocked also a declaration  condemning china for its security law  in hong kong so as you can see it's  been difficult for the european union to  to find to speak with one voice  so we will see if hungary and victor  orban  is considered a strong ally of vladimir  putin  will be on board but we at least can  can expect a statement released by the  majority  of member states okay let's talk then  about uh the influence of russia behind  this we'll hear now from the ceo of  ryanair that's uh this is michael  o'leary who says that they were security  agents  on the flight this was a case of  state-sponsored  uh it was a state-sponsored hijacker  state-sponsored piracy  i think it was very frightening for the  crew for the passengers and who were  held under armed guard had their bags  searched  um when it was clear it appears that the  intent of the russian authorities was to  remove a journalist and his traveling  companion  and you know we believe there was also  some uh kgb  agents off loaded off the aircraft as  well  alexander the presumption is that  president luka shankar wouldn't have  taken such a dramatic step without it  being signed off by the kremlin  so is this likely to affect the already  fraught relations between the eu and  russia  well that is something that we can  really assume i mean russia was the  reason for  eu leaders to come together and have the  dinner tonight in the first place to  discuss the relations with russia  that are being described as being at the  lowest  level uh in in decades even according to  your foreign policy chief  however there are always member states  arguing that it's important  to to try to work together with russia  because  the european union needs russia to solve  international crisis for example so uh  we'll see if they can have a common  position  on that what is clear is this that this  operation in minsk uh  could not happen without the support of  russia its secret service and  it's also clear that alexander  lukashenko could not  be able to hold on to power for such a  long time without vladimir putin's  support  okay thank you for that alexandra von  nerman in brussels  you

